# Force LF line endings for files that run as scripts inside Linux
# containers, regardless of the checkout platform's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with the common core.autocrlf=true
# converts LF to CRLF on checkout -- which silently breaks any POSIX shell
# script mounted into a container (a CRLF-corrupted `set -eu` line becomes
# `set -eu\r`, which `sh` rejects with "illegal option -", failing the
# script). Confirmed empirically: examples/n8n/scripts/n8n-init.sh, checked
# out fresh with core.autocrlf=true, fails compose.dev.yml's `n8n-init`
# step this exact way. See tests/test_repo_hygiene.py for the regression
# guard that checks tracked shell scripts never contain CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf
